<BaseLayout title="The Mew & Maple Notebook" description="Free printable resources for indoor-cat living: a seven-day activity planner, small-home checklist and an illustrated room-planning example.">
<section class="container resource-page"><p class="eyebrow">KEEP SOMETHING USEFUL</p><h1>The Mew &amp; Maple Notebook</h1><p class="lead">Small plans for better everyday living. Free to read, save and print, with no account or email required.</p>
<div class="notebook-grid"><section class="reading-panel" id="weekly-planner"><p class="eyebrow">TWO-PAGE PRINTABLE</p><h2>A week of better play</h2><p>Seven optional prompts and a roomy observation log. Choose, repeat or skip activities according to your cat.</p><ul><li>One gentle idea each day, from supervised play to quiet observation.</li><li>Space to note preferences and one change for next week.</li><li>No required exercise quota or feeding changes.</li></ul><a class="button" href="/downloads/7-day-indoor-cat-planner.pdf" download>Download the weekly planner (PDF)</a></section>
<section class="reading-panel" id="home-checklist"><p class="eyebrow">ONE-PAGE PRINTABLE</p><h2>One thoughtful home check</h2><p>Walk through rest, resources, routes, toys and everyday hazards. Pick one improvement you can actually make.</p><ul><li>Use across your whole home, not just a photographed corner.</li><li>Consider access and choice for each cat.</li><li>A planning prompt, not a safety certificate.</li></ul><a class="button" href="/downloads/small-home-cat-checklist.pdf" download>Download the home checklist (PDF)</a></section></div>
<section class="reading-panel"><p class="eyebrow">SMALL SPACE, BETTER DAYS</p><h2>See one room differently</h2><p>Explore a conceptual 3 × 4 m living room, with five marked zones and room to move. It is an original planning illustration, not a tested home installation.</p><a href="/notebook/small-room-plan/">Explore the small-room example →</a></section>
<section class="resource-sources"><h2>Before you try an activity</h2><p>Inspect toys and supports. Supervise unfamiliar activities and put strings or feather wands away after play. Let your cat opt out. Sudden or concerning changes in appetite, movement or behavior need veterinary advice.</p><p>These resources offer general planning ideas. No veterinary review or hands-on product testing is claimed.</p><h3>Background reading</h3><ul><li><a href="https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11383066/">AAFP/ISFM Feline Environmental Needs Guidelines (2013)</a></li><li><a href="https://www.vet.cornell.edu/departments-centers-and-institutes/cornell-feline-health-center/health-information/feline-health-topics/safe-toys-and-gifts">Cornell Feline Health Center: Safe Toys and Gifts</a></li></ul><p class="resource-date">Resource edition: October 18, 2026.</p></section></section></BaseLayout>
